1
00:00:00,610 --> 00:00:03,612
So viewing information about the model is of

2
00:00:03,644 --> 00:00:05,859
course helpful and interesting,

3
00:00:06,437 --> 00:00:09,841
but changing some settings with help of set

4
00:00:10,049 --> 00:00:11,366
can be even more interesting.

5
00:00:12,570 --> 00:00:15,652
Now if you type just /set, you get a list of

6
00:00:15,989 --> 00:00:17,434
things you can set.

7
00:00:18,269 --> 00:00:20,581
For example, you can set parameters like the

8
00:00:20,581 --> 00:00:21,078
temperature.

9
00:00:21,929 --> 00:00:24,016
You can also set a system message

10
00:00:24,675 --> 00:00:26,489
for this chat session then.

11
00:00:27,066 --> 00:00:29,437
And a couple of other things. For example,

12
00:00:29,789 --> 00:00:32,337
whether it automatically wraps words if they

13
00:00:32,369 --> 00:00:33,378
reach the end of line,

14
00:00:34,275 --> 00:00:36,966
whether you wanna enable JSON mode and force

15
00:00:37,175 --> 00:00:39,578
the model to always reply with data

16
00:00:39,578 --> 00:00:42,686
structured as JSON, which can be helpful if

17
00:00:42,686 --> 00:00:45,617
you plan on using the model response in some

18
00:00:46,050 --> 00:00:47,812
automated workflow or some other

19
00:00:47,812 --> 00:00:48,437
application,

20
00:00:49,174 --> 00:00:50,920
and a couple of other things as well.

21
00:00:51,593 --> 00:00:54,028
Set verbose can, for example, be interesting

22
00:00:54,140 --> 00:00:55,854
to get a bit more behind the scenes

23
00:00:55,854 --> 00:00:58,113
information when interacting with the model.

24
00:00:59,211 --> 00:01:01,302
So here I did set verbose mode.

25
00:01:02,235 --> 00:01:05,629
You can always disable it by setting quiet

26
00:01:05,629 --> 00:01:06,481
thereafter again.

27
00:01:07,398 --> 00:01:09,509
But with it being set, if you, for example,

28
00:01:09,638 --> 00:01:11,573
ask, what can you do for me?

29
00:01:12,944 --> 00:01:15,991
You'll get a response just as you're used

30
00:01:16,024 --> 00:01:16,169
to.

31
00:01:18,429 --> 00:01:20,601
But then at the end of this response, you

32
00:01:20,617 --> 00:01:23,033
get some summary statistics, some

33
00:01:23,065 --> 00:01:25,302
information about how this response was

34
00:01:25,335 --> 00:01:25,788
generated.

35
00:01:26,818 --> 00:01:28,728
You, for example, get the number of tokens

36
00:01:28,761 --> 00:01:31,024
that were generated per second, giving you

37
00:01:31,024 --> 00:01:33,754
some useful insights into the performance

38
00:01:33,754 --> 00:01:35,713
and the speed of this model on your system

39
00:01:36,532 --> 00:01:38,202
and some other pieces of information.

40
00:01:39,101 --> 00:01:40,738
Of course, you also might want to set a

41
00:01:40,738 --> 00:01:43,211
system message. And for that, you just type

42
00:01:43,372 --> 00:01:46,181
/set system and then any message of your

43
00:01:46,181 --> 00:01:47,000
choice, like

44
00:01:47,851 --> 00:01:49,344
always reply in

45
00:01:49,939 --> 00:01:50,324
rhymes

46
00:01:50,854 --> 00:01:51,657
no matter

47
00:01:52,449 --> 00:01:53,932
what the user

48
00:01:54,625 --> 00:01:55,140
asks you.

49
00:01:56,752 --> 00:01:59,073
So now I did set that system message and we

50
00:01:59,137 --> 00:02:01,103
can see it if I type show system.

51
00:02:02,135 --> 00:02:03,762
That proves that it has been set.

52
00:02:05,358 --> 00:02:06,196
And then of course

53
00:02:07,034 --> 00:02:09,474
I can send any other follow-up message and

54
00:02:09,474 --> 00:02:11,471
for example, ask it to summarize

55
00:02:12,380 --> 00:02:13,760
what you can do for me.

56
00:02:15,647 --> 00:02:17,915
And due to that system message, it goes

57
00:02:17,948 --> 00:02:19,546
ahead and does that in rhymes.

58
00:02:21,326 --> 00:02:23,294
Now if I quit this session, which you can

59
00:02:23,406 --> 00:02:24,229
always do with bye,

60
00:02:25,825 --> 00:02:28,083
and I then start a new one by running ollama

61
00:02:28,083 --> 00:02:30,066
run and then the model identifier again,

62
00:02:31,806 --> 00:02:34,254
you will see that this system message is now

63
00:02:34,608 --> 00:02:37,330
no longer set because the system message,

64
00:02:37,604 --> 00:02:39,908
when setting it with set system, is always

65
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:42,566
set for one specific chat session, not for

66
00:02:42,598 --> 00:02:43,420
the model itself.

67
00:02:44,048 --> 00:02:46,610
You can also set a system message for the

68
00:02:46,691 --> 00:02:48,533
overall model, But that's something we'll

69
00:02:48,533 --> 00:02:49,414
get back to later.

70
00:02:50,360 --> 00:02:52,250
Now, one other important thing you can

71
00:02:52,699 --> 00:02:55,119
adjust is the parameters of the model.

72
00:02:56,064 --> 00:02:58,147
For that, you can type set parameter.

73
00:02:59,269 --> 00:03:01,080
And if you hit enter, you'll get a list of

74
00:03:01,112 --> 00:03:02,538
the parameters you can set.

75
00:03:04,012 --> 00:03:06,320
For example, top K, top P,

76
00:03:07,105 --> 00:03:08,419
and the temperature.

77
00:03:08,948 --> 00:03:11,431
And these are of course the settings that

78
00:03:11,431 --> 00:03:14,364
control how creative or predictable the

79
00:03:14,364 --> 00:03:17,063
model is, and again, attached you'll find a

80
00:03:17,063 --> 00:03:19,843
link to a lecture where I explain these

81
00:03:19,875 --> 00:03:21,530
parameters in greater detail.

82
00:03:22,285 --> 00:03:24,856
But you can, for example, also set the

83
00:03:24,920 --> 00:03:28,038
context window here, the context size.

84
00:03:29,178 --> 00:03:31,267
As I explained before, depending on the

85
00:03:31,267 --> 00:03:33,918
model you're using, there is a maximum

86
00:03:34,175 --> 00:03:36,955
context window size supported by the model,

87
00:03:37,212 --> 00:03:39,462
but that's not necessarily the context

88
00:03:39,462 --> 00:03:40,811
window size that's active.

89
00:03:42,126 --> 00:03:44,164
And why is it not always using the maximum?

90
00:03:44,436 --> 00:03:46,586
Well, because all that space for all these

91
00:03:46,618 --> 00:03:49,169
tokens must be reserved in memory. And

92
00:03:49,266 --> 00:03:51,111
therefore you typically don't wanna

93
00:03:51,688 --> 00:03:54,464
set a huge context window size if you don't

94
00:03:54,464 --> 00:03:57,015
need it, because that will eat up a lot of

95
00:03:57,047 --> 00:03:59,967
memory and potentially may even need more

96
00:04:00,079 --> 00:04:02,020
memory than you got available on your

97
00:04:02,100 --> 00:04:02,566
system.

98
00:04:03,785 --> 00:04:05,935
But you can of course set parameter

99
00:04:06,752 --> 00:04:09,844
numctx to change the context window size

100
00:04:10,357 --> 00:04:12,248
that is assigned to this chat session

101
00:04:12,841 --> 00:04:14,651
and set it to 10,000.

102
00:04:15,564 --> 00:04:18,048
And now this session here would have a

103
00:04:18,048 --> 00:04:20,707
context window size of 10,000 tokens.

104
00:04:22,053 --> 00:04:24,456
But of course, as I just explained, it

105
00:04:24,456 --> 00:04:27,212
therefore also needs more memory space, more

106
00:04:27,565 --> 00:04:28,590
VRAM space.

107
00:04:29,648 --> 00:04:32,451
And that's essentially it for the most

108
00:04:32,451 --> 00:04:35,704
important settings. As you see, you You can

109
00:04:35,752 --> 00:04:39,247
of course set more things, more parameters,

110
00:04:39,503 --> 00:04:42,373
and especially top_k, top_p, and temperature

111
00:04:42,549 --> 00:04:44,857
are parameters you might wanna set according

112
00:04:45,050 --> 00:04:47,871
to those explanations I provided in that

113
00:04:47,919 --> 00:04:49,987
previous course section with that lecture

114
00:04:50,003 --> 00:04:52,552
you find attached. But most other things you

115
00:04:52,584 --> 00:04:54,828
can set are typically not things you'll need

116
00:04:54,828 --> 00:04:55,998
to change all the time.

117
00:04:56,912 --> 00:04:59,188
For example, the history here, which you can

118
00:04:59,316 --> 00:05:01,849
enable or disable, is not the chat history,

119
00:05:02,026 --> 00:05:03,228
which is what you could think.

120
00:05:03,789 --> 00:05:05,889
Instead, that is just the history of

121
00:05:05,889 --> 00:05:08,780
messages you sent, you can always recall by

122
00:05:08,780 --> 00:05:11,324
pressing the up and down arrow keys.

123
00:05:12,161 --> 00:05:13,819
That is the history that's being saved.

124
00:05:14,560 --> 00:05:16,408
And if you know that certain commands or

125
00:05:16,488 --> 00:05:18,368
messages you send to the model should not be

126
00:05:18,368 --> 00:05:20,795
included in that history, you could set no

127
00:05:20,923 --> 00:05:22,804
history before running a command.

128
00:05:23,366 --> 00:05:24,169
So if I now type

129
00:05:25,358 --> 00:05:25,840
hello,

130
00:05:26,483 --> 00:05:28,379
I'm sending this as a message to the model.

131
00:05:29,857 --> 00:05:31,657
And now if I quit that model

132
00:05:32,493 --> 00:05:35,027
and I restart it and I press the up arrow

133
00:05:35,027 --> 00:05:36,871
key, I don't get hello as a suggestion, but

134
00:05:36,871 --> 00:05:37,214
instead set no history.

135
00:05:39,456 --> 00:05:41,833
Which was the last command I executed before

136
00:05:41,865 --> 00:05:43,214
I disabled the history.

137
00:05:44,306 --> 00:05:48,754
So that will disable cross-session command

138
00:05:49,091 --> 00:05:51,934
memory, so to say. If you don't want

139
00:05:52,544 --> 00:05:54,503
Ollama to memorize certain commands or

140
00:05:54,503 --> 00:05:56,880
messages across sessions, you can set no

141
00:05:56,896 --> 00:05:59,176
history. Here I did re-enable it though.
